A||||41||Scott2M4||A test - can you guess what this noise is?||||1||
Z||000000||Scott2M4||01-10-2000||06:01 PM||||Around 2500 RPMs - both under load (running through the gears) and cruising in 5th gear, my '87 2.5 makes a sound like chains bouncing off of the pavement behind me (for those that have ever been pulling a trailer and had a safety chain drop off of the bumper and bounce/drag on the pavement).  It's not loud by any means but I've "trained" my ears to listen for anything!<P>Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||01-10-2000||06:06 PM||||<BR>Bummer... I thought you were going to have a WAV file loaded up.<P>I'll guess anyway. A really loose timing chain bouncing around and rubbing against the inside of the timing chain cover?||||reg||12
Z||000002||Patrick||01-10-2000||06:08 PM||||<BR>Or detonation?||||reg||5
Z||000003||DRH||01-10-2000||06:17 PM||||The 2.5 doesn't have a timing chain, just gears (at least on the 84, don't think it changed for 87)<P>My guess is something in or connected to the exhaust rattling around.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||01-10-2000||06:22 PM||||<BR>Ummmm.... I knew that. I was just testing you guys. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000005||87GT||01-11-2000||05:23 PM||||You know what, I have a noise like this in my 87GT that does that through acceleration.  I hear it also when I am just giving it gas as I go down the road.  It sounds like a low rattly chain sound.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||01-11-2000||05:38 PM||||It’s Gremlins under your car reeking havoc.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||01-11-2000||06:02 PM||||You might want to check the rubbers of the exhaust or the weld points of the thingy that holds these rubbers. Easiest way to check this is to see if you can move the exhaust pipes up and down.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mshill||01-11-2000||06:11 PM||||Thinking along the same lines as Cliff, I once had a spring from my exhaust loose and it would rattle depending on the engine vibration.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||01-11-2000||06:12 PM||||We're going to have to come up with a universal parts discription list for various Fiero parts and directions . Examples: what is the engine cover called hood? trunk? where is the left side of the engine  flywheel? plugs 2,4,6? front manifold trunk? pass comp?<BR>blue dingy thingy ,goink suppressor etc.<BR>I worry about stupid stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000010||WGraffiKs||01-11-2000||07:00 PM||||Check for loose heat shields on the engine.  There are a few of them on my 2.8L/V-6, but I don't know much about the 2.5's.  Even ONE missing screw on a shield could cause a "rattle" under the right conditions. <BR>Good Luck!! ||||reg||3
Z||000011||Ed||01-11-2000||08:51 PM||||The shop manual has abbreviations listed. I use the shop manual to describe parts.<P>I gets really interesting when you have an English sportscar. Then you have to translate English to English.  Boot = trunk, hood = convertible top, etc. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Harry||01-11-2000||10:21 PM||||My 85GT 2.8 clutch slave has a heat shield loose right behind it that I havent bothered to remove yet that sounds just like a chain draggin from a hitch tooooooooooooo......||||reg||6
Z||000013||terryk||01-11-2000||11:00 PM||||Clearly a mis-alignment between the Johnson valve and Miller quadrant sprague. Have they been phase-aligned lately?||||reg||1
Z||000014||william_thorniii||01-12-2000||12:34 AM||||Make sure to check the flux capacitor and while you are at it take some plutonium for the return trip.<P>Seriously though can a shield really sound like a chain?  I have heard them vibrate in virtually every manner possible, but never sound like a safety/tow chain.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000015||Phaeton||01-12-2000||12:54 AM||||Could be a sympathetic vibration.  Something fastened but flexible enough to resonate (bracket end or exhaust hanger) at 2500.  Can  you put it on stands and have somebody put it in gear and hold 2500 rpm while you look and listen?||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||01-12-2000||01:10 AM||||After I thought about it, my V6 factory original muffler could have been described that way when the guts fell apart. Pretty bad.<P>What does it take to make the sound stop?<P>Could be pinging, but it would go away if you let off the throttle some and it is unlikely to happen in all gears since the load changes.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Cliff Pennock||01-12-2000||02:44 AM||||I would run a full level-2 diagnostics if I were you.||||reg||1
Z||000018||DJRice||01-12-2000||09:29 AM||||Definitely run the level 2 diagnostics as Cliff suggested.  Pay particular attention to the phase variance of the field coils.  If these are not within specified tolerances, you could end up with fluctuations in the field density which will cause feedback to the plasma injectors.  That feedback could cause some resonation in the engine core which in turn will cause resonation in the containment field.  <P>(Which sounds alot like dragging a chain)||||reg||2
Z||000019||Raydar||01-12-2000||09:42 AM||||Naaaahhhh. It's just the muffler bearings.<BR>Later cars had bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Raydar||01-12-2000||09:45 AM||||...either that, or an exhaust system part or a heat shield.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||deceler8||01-12-2000||10:36 AM||||This may sound crazy, but check the rear brake calipers to be sure they're tight. I had a really mysterious clanging sound in the rear of my car that sounded similar, and it turned out to be a loose rear caliper assembly. <P>I tightened the bolts (T50 Torx, I believe) and sound went away.||||reg||1
Z||000022||dogtired||01-12-2000||12:45 PM||||I am always UP for a TEST!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Check your CAT!<BR>Crawl under with a rubber mallet and bang around, see if you can make it happen???<P>DID I PASS???   ||||reg||6
Z||000023||terryk||01-11-2000||01:47 PM||||To Scott2M4,<P>You're going to have to jump in here sometime and give us more information. The thread is slowly disintegrating into power-lounging and interior decoration tips. <P>And running the level 2 diagonostics is liable to just generate more transcription errors. Don't do it.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Scott2M4||01-11-2000||03:48 PM||||More info ...<P>I bought the car on 11/02/99.  Didn't start hearing the noise until I changed the oil a few days after that.  The previous owner ran 10w30 through it.  I switched over to Mobil 1 synthetic 5w30 (in fact, the overall noise level got a little higher after that but the engine seemed to run more smoothly).  The noise got a little more noticeable when I took the engine to 4700 RPMs during a temper tantrum a few weeks ago (don't lecture me!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).<P>I recall reading another thread where someone mentioned getting their floor mat stuck under the pedal which caused the engine to redline.  They started to notice a tick or a click or a ting or something.  I think it ended up being a lifter or something.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Patrick||01-11-2000||07:13 PM||||<BR>Well, now that you've said that, answer us this -<P>Does the sound occur while the car is standing still with the engine at 2500 RPM?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000026||Scott2M4||01-12-2000||09:41 AM||||Funny you should ask that, Patrick!<P>Last night, the noise was very noticeable coming home from work.  So, once I got home and parked the car, I opened the back, revved the engine to 2500 and listened ... and listened ... and listened ... I COULDN'T HEAR IT!<P>What gives!?||||reg||4
Z||000027||Patrick||01-12-2000||01:06 PM||||<BR>I guess we can rule out a lifter or anything else in the valve train.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Scott2M4||01-13-2000||07:57 AM||||Rule out the valve train and lifters? I hope you're right but, for some reason (and I have nothing to base this on), I just have this gut feeling it IS the valve train or lifters. Don't ask me why, I just do.<P>I guess I just wanted someone to say: "I had that same noise in my car.  Had to replace a lifter."  or "I had that same noise in my car.  Had to rebuild the top end of the motor!" ...||||reg||1
Z||000030||William Federle||01-13-2000||01:19 PM||||The thing about reving it to 2500 RPM while sitting still - it's not under load. That could make a difference.||||reg||1
Z||000031||87GTBro||01-13-2000||01:45 PM||||If you take that silly tape out of the stereo, you won't hear the noise anymore...||||reg||12
Z||000032||Patrick||01-13-2000||03:01 PM||||<BR>My experiences with bad valve trains (not on my Fiero) is that they are noisy all the time, load or no load. A really bad lifter produces a "clack, clack, clack" sound while at idle. When the motor is revved up, it's more like a loud rapid ticking. <P>Bad valve trains I've heard never sounded like a chain being dragged on the pavement.||||reg||1
Z||000033||William Federle||01-13-2000||07:03 PM||||I guess that it must not be a valve train noise then - since this happens while driving and not while sitting still. Maybe it has to do with something between the clutch and the wheels. Or it might be due to some other engine component since it seem to start with the change from 10W30 to 5W30.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 01-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||Patrick||01-13-2000||07:17 PM||||<BR>What happens if you're doing 30mph and you put the car in neutral and coast? Does the sound slow down as the engine RPM drops, or does the speed of the sound remain consistent with the speed of the car?||||reg||5
Z||000035||Scott2M4||01-14-2000||08:11 AM||||Well, I feel like an idiot.  Excuse me for a minute while I go ... <B>DUH!!!!!</B><P>The sound has been getting progressively louder.  This morning, it was loud enough that I could tell what it is ... it's an f-ing ping (or knock, or whatever you want to call it)!  At 50+ MPH, in 4th gear with the engine around 2500, it was so noisy I could hear it over the HVAC blower cranked all of the way up!  If I let off of the throttle, it went away.  The second I touched the throttle, it came back!<P>I guess it's time to do a search on pinging or knocking or whatever!<P>Sorry, folks.  But, this was fun, right!?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000036||DJRice||01-14-2000||08:50 AM||||If its a progressively louder knock, Its probably a bearing.......<P>That could be bad, or good, if you had <B><I>planned</I></B> on rebuilding the motor.||||reg||1
Z||000037||batboy||01-14-2000||09:57 AM||||If you are pinging that bad, the first thing to do is fill up with the highest octane gas you can find. I'd pull the spark plugs and check for carbon deposits. Clean or replace the plugs, but before you put them back in, spray some of that decarbonizing gunk down into the plug holes. Maybe this will help to clean out some of that carbon build-up in there which is often the cause of pinging. You may consider checking your timing and looking for vacuum leaks too.||||reg||1
Z||000038||ChadMan||01-14-2000||01:25 PM||||Scott, for some reason, the Iron Dukes really like 91+ octane gas!  Both my 84 and 88 pinged like crazy on 89 or less.  And severe detonation can cause damage to valves, pistons and/or head gaskets.<P>If you're already using hi-test, just tell me to shut the heck up.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000039||fiero56||01-14-2000||01:43 PM||||Sounds to me like Batboy(and Chad) nailed the problem. I've been wondering what could be making this noise all day. I kept coming up with dead end ideas, thinking of mechanical problems. Since Scott has an '87 I believe he has the DIS motor. How do you do the timing on one of these, I've never delt with one before and would like to learn.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||Scott2M4||01-14-2000||02:08 PM||||ChadMan - glad you brought it up.  From day one, I used Shell 93 octane.  Then, when gas prices went through the roof and they wanted $1.50+/gallon for premium, I switched to 87 (still $1.30+/gallon - AUGH!!!).  When I didn't notice any degradation in performance and, believe it or not, did notice a 3-4 MPG improvement in mileage, I decided to stick with it.  Sounds like I need to go back to 93.  I do plan to take batboy's advice regarding decarbonization.<P>fiero56 - thanks for bringing up the timing question.  How do you set the timing on the DIS motor?||||reg||1
Z||000041||DRH||01-14-2000||02:13 PM||||Well looks like Patrick WON!!! He mentioned detonation (pinging) way back in the second reply  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000042||Patrick||01-14-2000||05:41 PM||||<BR>Thanks David, I wasn't going to say anything, but... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>In answer to another question raised, there is NO timing adjustment on the DIS engine.||||reg||14
